# Gate review: python safepoint slice 2 (base 69e58662, uncommitted)

Scope: read-only static review of the diff + `test/yang/python/antlr/safepoint_programs.cljc` against the two ruling files and §8.5.2. No tests run, nothing edited.

## What checks out

- **`:base` mechanism matches the converged ruling.** `py/ctx` record with `:handlers :depth :base :frame`; `py/abs-depth` = base+depth (prelude.cljc:140-145); escapes restore the saved record with current `:base` kept (`py/restore!`, prelude.cljc:143-145); `py/crossing-base` = 1 + resumer's absolute depth (prelude.cljc:312-315); resume rebases `(assoc (get c :ctx) :base (py/crossing-base g))` (prelude.cljc:307-309); crossings out restore the caller's record whole (yield-raw :350, gen-exit :329). The draft sentence the ruling replaced ("a generator owns its whole record…") is gone from the doc.
- **79/99 pinning is right, and statically verified.** The only `:call` mark is the function-object lambda (lower.cljc:1077); a generator's body lambda `(fn [gen-sym] …)` is unmarked, and `py.b/next`/`print` are base-prelude `py/make-function`s the stage never rewrites — so `down(19,g)` = 20 frames, crossing at depth 20, base 21, `probe` → 79; from the top, base 1 → 99; top-level probe → 100; gen2's rebase + activation-relative escape → 99 not 79. The pinned set `["79" "99" "100" "79" "100" "79" "99" "100"]` is internally consistent and fails under the rejected first-resumer rule (79,79), so the test can detect the old bug. The `unwind` program's 99 (`h()` refuses only at frame 100's depth-100 finally, then succeeds 99 times down the stack) also verifies by construction.
- **Limit rules.** `py.sp/enter` raises only when base+d > limit ⇒ n calls run, n+1th raises; raise precedes the ctx write (safepoint.cljc:83-90). Setter: bool accepted (`py/int?` via `py/numeric?`, prelude.cljc:113-117,867), <1 ⇒ verbatim CPython ValueError, ≤ current absolute depth ⇒ RecursionError before the cell write (old limit preserved), non-int ⇒ TypeError (message unpinned per ruling) (safepoint.cljc:99-111). `RecursionError` under `RuntimeError` in the base prelude (prelude.cljc:1709-1714), so it exists naive and under no-op hooks — e2e pins exactly that.
- **Depth accounting invariants.** Handler frames grew to `[kind payload rest n depth]`; `frame-depth` still reads n, `unwind-to` compares n, `pop-frame` restores the frame's push depth so a finally thunk runs at its recorded depth (prelude.cljc:146-174) — the `unwind` fin-test's 99 depends on it. `py.sp/return` decrements only on normal exit; `call-ec` early return flows through it; escapes restore, never decrement; the refused `py.sp/enter` leaves depth un-incremented. Module body uncounted; `run-module` resets the whole record (prelude.cljc:1681).
- **Cross-host.** No new reader conditionals anywhere (the one in the test helper keeps `:cljd` first); no float unary minus, no dynamic keywords, no deftype/protocol/private-mutable-field surface; `state-definitions`' per-element quotes + `(list 'cell/new default-recursion-limit)` interpolate the default at Clojure load time without nested-quote surprises; nothing added calls the runtime reader, so the cljd whitespace-before-closer reader bug (docs/cljd-bug.md Bug 5) is out of reach; no `py.rt/handlers` stragglers.
- **Hygiene.** cljstyle check clean on all seven files; clj-kondo 0 errors/0 warnings. The packets in `safepoint_programs.cljc` are pinned to their docstring sources by `portable-packets-are-the-parsers-test` (e2e_test.clj:684-691), so tests cannot silently run a different program than documented. All three slice-1 consumers of `hooks/profile` got the `py.sp/return` no-op added; no other consumer exists.

## Findings

**P2** | src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:283-311 | The gpt ruling's normative requirement — "Check the effective depth when resuming, even if execution reaches another yield without an intervening function call. An admission failure must leave the suspended generator and caller context intact" — is not implemented. `py/gen-switch` performs no limit comparison on either live path (`py/gen-start` at :306, resume at :307-309); the only check in the system is `py.sp/enter` (src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/safepoint.cljc:87), which runs solely at stage-placed call sites. A call-free stretch between yields (`def g():\n x = 1\n while True:\n x = x + 1\n yield x`) resumed by `next(g)` while the resumer sits at the limit runs unbounded where CPython raises RecursionError at every resume entry. §8.5.2's new paragraph (docs/design/yang.antlr.md:1594-1597) frames the limit purely as call counting and omits it too. | In `py/gen-switch`, on both live paths, before any write to `g` or `py.rt/ctx` (caller-ctx is still the current record there, so `py/raise` unwinds the caller and leaves the generator :created/:suspended intact): `(if (< (cell/get py.sp/limit) (+ 1 (py/abs-depth (cell/get py.rt/ctx)))) (py/raise-new py.b/RecursionError …) …)`. Compatible with every pinned expectation (21≤100, 1≤100). Add the 8.5.2 sentence and a regression: call-free generator resumed at the limit ⇒ RecursionError, then resumable from shallower depth.

**P3** | docs/design/yang.antlr.md:1579-1581 | The pre-existing depth paragraph still reads "an escape restores the whole record saved at capture", one paragraph above the slice-2 refinement "comes back with the current `:base` kept" (:1592-1593). The diff is purely additive; the ruling's sentence was to *replace* the old depth claim, and the two now contradict each other on `:base`. | Trim the old clause to "an escape restores the record saved at capture (slice 2 refines this below)" or fold the `:base` caveat into it.

**P3** | test/yang/python/antlr/safepoint_test.cljc:536-643 | Four regression cases the rulings enumerate are absent, all expressible today: shallow-first-then-deep-second resumption (the implemented test only does deep→shallow; gpt §2 asks for both directions), nested *active* generators each counted once (e.g. an outer generator that resumes an inner via `next` while itself suspended — statically the base stack gives 98 from the top, untested), `gen.throw`/`close` with finally at the current resumption base (gen2 covers an in-body raise only), and a try exited by normal return across differing resume depths (gen2 covers the exception variant). Note the fable ruling's `yield from` additive chain is *blocked*, not omitted: the lowering refuses `yield from` (src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:1356) — record it as deferred. | Add the four programs to `safepoint_programs.cljc` (the parser-check test keeps them honest) with their pinned counts.

**P3** | src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/safepoint.cljc:85,92 | The file had zero lines over 80 columns at base; these two (85 and 92 cols) break its norm — cljstyle doesn't enforce length, but "where practical" is satisfied here. Also: 31 over-80 lines in `test/yang/python/antlr/safepoint_programs.cljc` vs 1 in its sibling fixture `lower_portable_test.cljc`, and the 105-col comment at prelude.cljc:273. Lines added to files that already carry long lines (prelude, the test files) are within their established style. | Break the two message strings onto continuation lines; reflow the packet data to the sibling's width; wrap the comment.

No P0/P1 findings. The slice matches the converged ruling and §8.5.2 on every point the task lists except the P2 resume-admission gap; the P2 plus the missing tests are the right bar for "lanes green" before the orchestrator re-runs and commits.
